{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "04-determinants.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Determinants",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Compute determinants, see that the determinant of a product is the product of determinants, and recognise a matrix with no inverse.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Determinant of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n> $\\det \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} = ad - bc$: the signed area of the parallelogram the columns span.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let A = [1, 2; 3, 4]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det(A)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "The unit square is spanned by $e_1$ and $e_2$. A matrix sends it to the parallelogram its columns span, and every other square of the grid to a copy of that: so the determinant is the factor the matrix scales **every** area by.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -3.6, 5.2, -1.4, 3.6\nlet M = (1 - t)*[1, 0; 0, 1] + t*[3, 1; 1, 2]\nG0 = grid([1, 0; 0, 1]) faint color 6\nG = grid(M) color 2\nSq = poly(0, M*[1; 0], M*[1; 1], M*[0; 1]) color 1\nNb = poly(0, M*[0; 1], M*[-1; 1], M*[-1; 0]) color 5\nE1 = arrow(0, M*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nL1 = label(M*[1; 0], \"e_1\") color 3\nE2 = arrow(0, M*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nL2 = label(M*[0; 1], \"e_2\") color 4\nD = value(det(M), \"\\text{area} = \\det = \")\n> show G0, Sq, Nb, E1, L1, E2, L2, D | The unit square, spanned by $e_1$ (green) and $e_2$ (pink), and the square beside it: area $1$ each.\n> show G; play t to 1 in 4s | Apply $\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$. The square becomes the parallelogram its columns span, of area $3 \\cdot 2 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 5$.\n> | The square beside it, and every square of the grid, grows by the same factor $5$. So does any shape, made of small enough squares.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A determinant can be negative. Move the plane from $I$ to $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and watch the order of $e_1$ and $e_2$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -1.6, 4, -0.9, 3.6\nlet M = (1 - t)*[1, 0; 0, 1] + t*[1, 2; 2, 1]\nG0 = grid([1, 0; 0, 1]) faint color 6\nG = grid(M) color 2\nSq = poly(0, M*[1; 0], M*[1; 1], M*[0; 1]) color 1\nE1 = arrow(0, M*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nL1 = label(M*[1; 0], \"e_1\") color 3\nE2 = arrow(0, M*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nL2 = label(M*[0; 1], \"e_2\") color 4\nD = value(det(M), \"\\det = \")\n> show G0, G, Sq, E1, L1, E2, L2, D | Turning counterclockwise from $e_1$ you meet $e_2$.\n> play t to 0.5 in 3s | The columns lean toward each other, and the square flattens to a segment: determinant $0$.\n> play t to 1 in 3s | Then it opens again, turned over: now $e_2$ is met turning *clockwise* from $e_1$. The area is $3$ and the sign records the flip: $\\det = -3$. The lesson's $A$, with $\\det A = -2$, turns the plane over too.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([2, 1, 0; 1, 3, 1; 0, 1, 2])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For a $3 \\times 3$ matrix the determinant is the factor volumes scale by.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Products",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Determinant of a product\n> $\\det(AB) = \\det A \\cdot \\det B$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let B = [0, 1; 1, 0]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det(A*B)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det(A)*det(B)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "When the determinant is zero",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Invertibility\n> A square matrix has an inverse exactly when its determinant is not zero. A zero determinant means the columns lie on one line (or plane): the area they span is flat.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([1, 2; 2, 4])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -3.5, 4.5, -1.2, 5.2\nlet M = (1 - t)*[1, 0; 0, 1] + t*[1, 2; 2, 4]\nG0 = grid([1, 0; 0, 1]) faint color 6\nK = line(0, [1, 2]) dashed color 6\nG = grid(M) color 2\nSq = poly(0, M*[1; 0], M*[1; 1], M*[0; 1]) color 1\nE1 = arrow(0, M*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nE2 = arrow(0, M*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nD = value(det(M), \"\\det = \")\n> show G0, G, Sq, E1, E2, D | The plane, and the matrix moving from $I$ to $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n> show K; play t to 1 in 4s | Its second column is twice its first, so both land on one line, and so does every point of the plane: the square is flattened, its area $0$.\n> | Squashed onto a line, the plane cannot be unfolded again: many points went to each point of the line, and no matrix can tell them apart. That is why a zero determinant means no inverse.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 2; 2, 4])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The reduced form has a row of zeros: the second column is twice the first.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([2, 1; 5, 3])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute the determinant.",
      "hints": [
        "$ad - bc$ with $a = 2,\\ b = 1,\\ c = 5,\\ d = 3$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([3, 6; 1, 2])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute the determinant. Is the matrix invertible?",
      "hints": [
        "Is one column a multiple of the other?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([1, 0, 0; 0, 2, 0; 0, 0, 3])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute the determinant of this diagonal matrix.",
      "hints": [
        "For a diagonal matrix it is the product of the diagonal."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> The determinant is the factor a matrix scales area by, negative when it turns the plane over; it multiplies across products (scaling by one factor, then another), and it is zero exactly when the plane is flattened and the matrix cannot be undone.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
